Dualayer Hierarchy Standard (DHS)
=================================

A layered filesystem hierarchy in which musl and GNU libc run side by side.

DHS defines where the files of an operating system live when the system is
assembled from layers (a self-sufficient base, a layer of installed
software and a boot layer), and how two C libraries, musl and glibc, are
installed on the same system without conflicting. "Dualayer" is dual (two
C libraries) and layer (a system built from layers).

  Version          1.0 (October 2026)
  PDF              https://github.com/Zainium-Dynamics/dualayer-hierarchy-standard/releases/download/latest/dualayer-hierarchy-standard.pdf
  Reference        Zainium OS
  License          CC BY-SA 4.0 (see LICENSE)


Why
---

The Filesystem Hierarchy Standard describes one shared tree: the vendor's
software and the user's software both go into /usr, and one C library
serves every program. That makes three things hard: keeping what the
vendor ships apart from what the user installs, upgrading the base as one
unit instead of file by file while it runs, and having a second C library,
because /lib, /lib64, /usr/include and the loader path assume there is
only one.

DHS gives every part of the system its own layer with a single owner,
composes the layers into one view at boot with OverlayFS, and gives each
C library a directory of its own.


The layout
----------

  /
  ├── overlayer/
  │   ├── syshub/        system layer: the base (the composed view at run time)
  │   │   ├── bin/ sbin/ libexec/ lib/ include/ share/ etc/ var/
  │   │   ├── <arch>-<vendor>-linux-musl/   primary C library
  │   │   └── drivers/   kernel modules and firmware
  │   ├── userland/      software the user installs
  │   │   ├── union/     files of installed packages (the upper layer)
  │   │   │   ├── var/   variable data of installed software
  │   │   │   └── <arch>-<vendor>-linux-gnu/   secondary C library
  │   │   └── work/  registry/
  │   ├── ignition/      kernels, initramfs, boot loader (read-only)
  │   ├── agents/        instructions for AI agents (optional)
  │   └── .syshub-pending/   a staged base upgrade
  ├── home/  root/  tmp/  run/  dev/  proc/  sys/
  └── var -> /overlayer/syshub/var

There is no /usr, /bin, /sbin, /lib, /lib64, /etc or /boot at the root,
not even as symbolic links.

  Layer        DHS name              Changed by                 Zainium OS
  System       /overlayer/syshub     base upgrades, at boot     syshub
  Userland     /overlayer/userland   the package manager        zexlib
  Ignition     /overlayer/ignition   upgrades, the installer    zaisys

A distribution may give the userland and ignition layers names of its own,
as long as the structure inside them is unchanged and the names are
documented in /overlayer. At boot the initramfs applies a staged base
upgrade, then mounts the system view at /overlayer/syshub with the system
layer below and the userland above. Every file on disk belongs to exactly
one layer, and deleting the userland leaves a base that still boots.


musl and glibc side by side
---------------------------

Every distribution chooses its primary C library, the one the base is
built against; the other one is the optional secondary. Each lives in a
directory named after its target triplet. Design A is musl primary (Zainium
OS, with glibc as a userland package), design B is glibc primary.

  - The shared bin, lib and include belong to the primary C library.
  - Every program names the loader of the C library it was linked against.
  - The secondary loader searches only its own lib, never the shared one.
  - Both read users, hosts, DNS and the time zone from /overlayer/syshub/etc.
  - Unprefixed compilers belong to the primary; the secondary's compiler
    has its triplet name and its own pkg-config path.

So there are no two loaders competing for /lib and /lib64, no headers of
one library compiled against the other's libc, and no program loading a
library built for the other. Binaries that ask for /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2
or /lib/ld-musl-x86_64.so.1 are started through the installed loader of the
same C library.


Software built for FHS systems
------------------------------

Scripts with #!/usr/bin/env, programs that open /etc/passwd and binaries
from other distributions keep working: FHS paths that do not exist are
resolved to their DHS place when they are used, and packages from other
distributions are adapted when they are installed.


Reading and building
--------------------

The standard is DocBook 5 in xml/, one file per chapter: intro, principles,
root, overlayer, composition, libc, compat, packages and appendix.

  make valid      validate against the DocBook 5 schema
  make html       one page per chapter, build/dhs/html/
  make single     one page, build/dhs/single/index.html
  make pdf        the print edition, build/dhs/dhs.pdf (needs WeasyPrint)

It needs make, xsltproc and xmllint; the schemas, stylesheets, fonts and
logos are in docbook/. CI builds the HTML and the PDF for every change
(artifact "dhs") and publishes the PDF of main on the "latest" release.


